        <!-- SECTION 2: What Is Western Fine Art? -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>What Is Western Fine Art?</h2>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Western fine art is author-driven work engaging the American West with intentional composition, narrative depth, and artistic restraint—regardless of medium.</strong></p>

        <p>Across mediums, Western fine art is unified by three factors:</p>

        <ul style="list-style:none; padding-left:0; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;"><strong>Authorship - Intent  -  Compositional Restraint</strong></li>
          </ul>

        <p>It is work created to be experienced as art first—not documentation, not décor, not nostalgia.</p>

        <p>The subject matter may include frontier architecture, cowboy life, open landscapes, Native American culture, or the tension between settlement and wilderness. But subject alone does not make something fine art.</p>

        <p>Intent of the work does. Execution does. And most importantly, vision does.</p>

        <p>Where fine art diverges from commercial or documentary work is in its refusal to perform. It does not pander to expectation. It does not chase trend. It exists on its own terms, anchored in tradition but not imprisoned by it.</p>

        <CornerstoneGalleryDoor id="i-7hD9CCh" galleryPath="/Galleries/Painterly-Fine-Art-Photography/Facing-History/Wild-West/Native-Americans/NA-Color" alt="Native American figure at the water's edge rendered with dignity and restraint" caption="Presence and continuity held through authored composition." href="/Galleries/Painterly-Fine-Art-Photography/Facing-History/Wild-West/Native-Americans/NA-Color/all" label="Browse Native American Portraits" align="left" />

        <aside class="inline-art-callout inline-art-callout--right">
